  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Myanmar committed genocide against Rohingya minority, top UN court rules",
      "link": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c804wzp20ddvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "description": "The unprecedented International Court of Justice verdict concerns a 2017 military crackdown that killed thousands.",
      "source_name": "BBC World News",
      "published": "2026-11-19T20:07:02"
    },
    {
      "title": "Rohingya genocide: What was said in court and what happens now?",
      "link": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/videos/c60qj10j0ld5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "description": "The verdict marks the first time a state has ever been found to have committed genocide by an international court.",
      "source_name": "BBC World News",
      "published": "2026-11-19T17:35:05"
    },
    {
      "title": "Myanmar ruling gives new relevance to genocide convention and is ‘full of messages’ for Israel",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/nov/19/myanmar-ruling-new-relevance-genocide-convention-messages-israel",
      "description": "<p>Hailed as ‘a rare moment of justice’, judges’ decision sets out a roadmap for assessing the question of intent</p><p>The <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/19/genocide-rohingya-international-court-of-justice-ruling-myanmar\">international court of justice (ICJ) verdict</a> making Myanmar the first country in history to be found guilty of committing genocide does not just deliver justice for Rohingya victims. It also gives the 1948 <a href=\"https://treaties.un.org/doc/publication/unts/volume%2078/volume-78-i-1021-english.pdf\">genocide convention</a> new relevance in governing the actions of nation states.</p><p>In their nine votes to six majority ruling, the panel of judges in The Hague set out a detailed roadmap for how the convention should be applied to real-world situations in which a government is accused of committing genocide against a whole population group, especially when it comes to assessing the question of intent.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/nov/19/myanmar-ruling-new-relevance-genocide-convention-messages-israel\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T17:14:55"
    },
    {
      "title": "‘Everyone struggled for this justice’: tears in court as Rohingya vindicated in Myanmar genocide ruling",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/nov/19/everyone-struggled-for-this-justice-tears-in-court-as-rohingya-vindicated-in-myanmar-genocide-ruling",
      "description": "<p>Relief in the immediate aftermath of a landmark ruling, but what happens next is in the hands of governments around the world</p><p>There were tears in the halls of The Hague in the Netherlands and an eruption of applause in the refugee camps of Bangladesh as the scattered communities of Rohingya watched the <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/19/genocide-rohingya-international-court-of-justice-ruling-myanmar\">UN’s top court rule the Myanmar military</a> had committed genocide against them.</p><p>For almost a decade, the Rohingya have awaited this moment; recognition from the international court of justice (ICJ) that Myanmar in 2017 intentionally tried to destroy the mostly Muslim minority group through a campaign of mass killings and rape. More than 10,000 Rohingya were killed and 700,000 fled to neighbouring Bangladesh.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2026/nov/19/everyone-struggled-for-this-justice-tears-in-court-as-rohingya-vindicated-in-myanmar-genocide-ruling\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T16:15:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "Myanmar committed genocide against Rohingya minority, world’s top court rules",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/19/genocide-rohingya-international-court-of-justice-ruling-myanmar",
      "description": "<p>Judgment finds ‘intent to destroy a substantial part of the Rohingya population’ after case seen as critical test for dealing with most serious of state crimes</p><p>Myanmar committed genocide against its Rohingya minority during a 2017 military campaign, the world’s highest court has ruled, in a historic and unprecedented decision.</p><p>The judgment from the international court of justice (ICJ) – which has never previously found a state responsible for genocide – ends a seven-year legal battle that was seen as a critical global test for dealing with the most serious of all state crimes.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/19/genocide-rohingya-international-court-of-justice-ruling-myanmar\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-19T13:52:10"
    },
